## Competitor and SEO Quick Scan

**Existing coverage landscape:**
Major consulting firms (BCG, McKinsey, Deloitte, Gartner) publish AI strategy guides that are comprehensive, vendor-neutral, and organized around maturity models. They are well-sourced but generic — they could apply to any company in any industry. Harvard Business Review publishes adjacent think pieces on AI adoption culture. LinkedIn thought leaders publish "my five AI tips" listicles.

**What existing content misses:**
- The departmental maturity framing (vs. company-wide strategy) is underrepresented
- Hybrid intelligence with actual failure-rate numbers is rarely presented in plain business language
- Token economics as a strategic (not IT) concern is almost entirely absent from business-level AI strategy content
- The "leaders fail first" inversion of leadership norms is not common in enterprise AI content
- The psychological identity disruption angle is occasionally touched on but almost never quantified or structured

**How this article differs:**
This article is practitioner-voiced, first-person, experience-led, and willing to say that most companies are getting this wrong in two specific measurable ways. It names a dollar-range cost problem (500–2,000/month per super user), a specific cost multiplier (130x between cheapest and most expensive model), and a specific failure-rate data set. That combination of specificity is rare in this content category.

**Question-based H2s for featured snippet / AI overview capture:**
- "Why do most AI projects fail to make it out of pilot?"
- "How do you build an AI strategy by department?"

**Primary search intent:** Business leaders (VP Engineering, CTO, COO, CDO) searching for why their AI initiatives are stalling or how to structure an AI strategy that survives contact with the organization. Secondary intent: practitioners looking for a diagnostic framework to bring to leadership.

