**H2: The psychology problem your AI project budget doesn't account for**

Section purpose: Name the identity disruption that turns well-intentioned AI adoption into quiet sabotage. This is the section most likely to resonate with leaders who've already bought the tools and are confused by low adoption.

- H3: Why employees become saboteurs without meaning to
  - "I am a software developer" — identity attached to the task, not the output
  - Moving the work = moving the identity. Resistance is not irrational.
  - Forced AI adoption produces an army of people who will point out every mistake the AI makes
- H3: Two failure modes — over-reliance and under-reliance
  - Over-reliance: believing AI is magic, skipping quality checks, not learning the new error patterns AI introduces (different from human error patterns)
  - Under-reliance: hyper-specific prompting, re-prompting every time instead of capturing learnings into agents, treating AI as a search engine rather than a configurable system
  - The fix for each is different and requires deliberate people-side intervention
- H3: Start by eating the parts of the job nobody wanted anyway
  - Find the work the team already resents — expense reports, status updates, document searches
  - Let AI improve that first. "Oh, AI is a friend of mine."
  - Then move to expanding and amplifying the work people actually value
  - From "write the code" to "help the customer deliver an outcome"

